  "query_preview": "..Moisture has increased from south to north over our\nregion over the past 24 hours, as deeper moisture arrives from \ndistant Hurricane Marie (well offshore the southern tip of Baja).\nPrecipitable water values rose from 0.73\" Friday afternoon to\n0.93\" Saturday afternoon on the sounding here at Bellemont. This\nwill continue today as additional moisture gets pulled northward\ntoward a shortwave approaching the southern California coast\nthrough the afternoon. Although isolated light showers have\ndeveloped early this morning from Flagstaff northwestward, model\nguidance still indicates the more steady band of rain later this\nafternoon will set up west of our forecast area, from southern\nCalifornia through the Las Vegas area and into far NW Arizona/SW\nUtah. This area coincides with the better forcing from the\nshortwave and higher values of low level moisture. For our\nforecast area, the best chance for rain today will be over\nnorthwest Coconino County, closest to the main band, with some\nupslope showers also possible due to moderate southwest flow over\nhigher terrain of the Kaibab Plateau. Most other areas will see\nplenty of clouds limiting instability, so only isolated showers or\nthunderstorms are forecast today. By later tonight through Labor\nDay, the northwest US low pressure system will shift eastward and\nintroduce a drier southwest flow to most of our area with rain\nchances less than 10%. The exception will be southern Apache\nCounty around the White Mountains where deeper moisture lingers\nand a 30-60% chance of showers/t-storms continues. \n\nTuesday through Saturday - a large ridge is forecast to stretch\nfrom Arizona to the east coast Tue/Wed but then gradually become\ncentered over the southwest US Thursday onward. This will be\nfavorable for a return of monsoon moisture from the east starting\nTuesday and continuing through Wednesday, with 20-50% chances for\nstorms along the higher terrain. The forecast trend of building \nthe high right over our area Thursday and possibly even west of us\nby Friday continues, which could result in a downturn in storm \nchances along with hotter temperatures - this scenario is still\nuncertain so watch for updates for later next week.",
